net.tunnel
  Run a Cloudflare Tunnel connector, for a website with no public port
  Applies to this host.

Why
  A web server on ports 80 and 443 is open to every scanner on the internet. A
  Cloudflare Tunnel connector opens outbound connections to Cloudflare's edge
  and receives visitors through them, so the host needs no inbound port at all;
  firewall.inbound can then leave 80 and 443 closed.

Params (in the module's profile table)
  provider (default "cloudflare")
      The tunnel provider; "cloudflare" is the only one.
  token_file (default "")
      A file holding the token of a tunnel created in the Cloudflare dashboard,
      owned by root with mode 0600, not a symlink, in directories only root can
      write to. The connector's unit copies it at each start
      (`LoadCredential=`); vps-ready never reads it. After replacing it, run
      `sudo systemctl restart vps-ready-cloudflared`. Empty: cloudflared is
      installed, and no connector runs.
  auto_update (default true)
      Let unattended-upgrades (updates.auto) install cloudflared's updates; a
      path unit then restarts the connector on the new binary, which drops
      visitors' connections for a moment.

Fixes these audit controls
  none
